<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Angoche</h1><p class="st-meta">1485 CE – 1910 CE (approx.) · capital Angoche</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Swahili Muslim sultanate on the Mozambique coast that traded inland past the Portuguese and resisted colonial conquest until 1910.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Angoche was founded on coastal islands in the late 15th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> settlers linked to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa</a>. It was already an established trading town when the Portuguese arrived, and it served as an alternative outlet for gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> valley, bypassing Portuguese-controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique-island">Mozambique Island</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan ruled with a council of leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> lineages, and the town was a centre of Islamic learning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods for the coast. Trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and, in the 19th century, slaves for French and Brazilian buyers made it wealthy. Under Musa Mohamed Sahib Quanto in the 1860s Angoche expanded inland among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makua">Makua</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Portuguese forces occupied Angoche in 1861, but resistance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makua">Makua</a> leaders continued for decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Farelay fought on until his capture in 1910, when the sultanate was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-angoche.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Angoche" width="320" height="482"><figcaption>Image: AbdurRahman AbdulMoneim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultanate_of_Kilwa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1485 CE – 1910 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Angoche</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Musa Quanto, Farelay</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili, Koti</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mozambique</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Angoche, Angoxa</dd></dl></aside></div>

